--TEST--
a deployment granted `net.connect` may not bind, the refusal names the endpoint it asked for, and a bind takes an IP address given as a string literal rather than a host name — `rule:security/net-listen-is-a-separate-grant-from-net-connect`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The other direction of the same split. This deployment may reach the
// loopback host — the address policy exception is written for it and the host
// is granted by name — and it still may not open a port there.
try {
    Core\Net::listen("127.0.0.1", 8080);
    echo "bound it", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The refusal above names the endpoint rather than the address, because
// `net.listen`'s entries are `address:port` literals matched exactly: what a
// bind risks is occupying a port another service expects, and that is a
// question about the pair.
//
// And a bind resolves nothing. A hostname is refused where it is written
// rather than looked up, since a name answering two addresses could not be the
// one endpoint an operator granted.
try {
    Core\Net::listen("localhost", 8080);
    echo "bound it", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `net.connect` and its address exception, and no `net.listen` at all — the
# widest outbound grant a deployment can write for this host, buying nothing
# inbound.
[capabilities.net]
connect = ["127.0.0.1"]
internal = ["127.0.0.1"]
--EXPECT--
Core\Net::listen needs the capability `net.listen` for 127.0.0.1:8080, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.net]`
Core\Net::listen: `localhost` is not an IP address. A bind needs an endpoint, and a hostname is not one
